<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need an Umbrella.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Identify the main ideas and details in short narratives.<br>reading2: Respond creatively to stories read.<br>phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can locate key information about the weather in readings (e.g., 'It is rainy in June.').<br>Can respond creatively by identifying weather words in short stories (e.g., 'rain, wet, puddle.').<br>Can apply phonemic awareness and alphabet knowledge to read and spell one-syllable words related to weather (e.g., /sk/ /aɪ/ in 'sky,' /m/ /ʌdi/ in 'muddy').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or large pictures of weather items and conditions (umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle, rain, wet, sunny, cloudy, windy). Students identify them by saying the word. The teacher then asks, "What do you see?" and "What do you need when it's rainy?" to activate prior knowledge. Students point to pictures and respond with single words or short phrases (e.g., "umbrella!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of "rain" and clearly pronounces /r/ in 'rain,' having students repeat. Then, holds up a picture of "puddle" and clearly pronounces /p/ in 'puddle,' having students repeat. The teacher models how to connect words to pictures. The teacher reads a very short sentence like "It is raining. I need an umbrella." and points to the words as they are read. This models how to follow text visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'rain' for sunny days or rainy days?" (Expected answer: "Rainy days!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we need an umbrella when it is raining?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative (e.g., a simple story about a character named Leo and his day during the rainy season). The text will be simple and repetitive, using target vocabulary (rain, puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, wet, muddy) and grammar (present simple, present continuous, WH-questions). Example text excerpt: "It is rainy today! The sky is grey. Leo wants to go outside. He needs his raincoat and boots. He sees a big puddle. Splash! What does Leo do? He likes to puddle-jump!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify the main ideas and details in short narratives; Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud once, pointing to words as she reads.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the weather like in the story?" (Students respond: "Rainy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False activity:</b> The teacher reads sentences from the story or similar sentences. Students raise a green card for "True" and a red card for "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is sunny today in the story." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo wears his boots." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students are given pictures (e.g., a child wearing boots, an umbrella, a puddle). The teacher reads short phrases from the story (e.g., "He needs his boots," "He sees a big puddle"). Students point to the corresponding picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will read sentences and texts, identify specific words related to weather and clothing, and practice phonemic awareness for one-syllable words. (Grammar: Present simple, Present continuous, WH-questions; Vocabulary: puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, wet, rainy, muddy; Pronunciation: /r/ in 'rain', /p/ in 'puddle', phonemic awareness for 'sky', 'muddy').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Fill-in-the-Blanks Story (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a printed copy of the story from Stage 2 with key vocabulary words missing (e.g., "It is ____ today! Leo needs his ____ and ____."). A word bank with target vocabulary (rainy, raincoat, boots, puddle, umbrella) is provided. Students read the story individually and fill in the blanks using words from the word bank. The teacher circulates, offering help and checking for correct spelling and word choice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Read and Match Weather Sentences (Semi-controlled):</b> Students are given a worksheet with sentences on one side and matching pictures on the other. Sentences include: "It is raining," "I need an umbrella," "He likes to puddle-jump," "The sky is gloomy," "She wears her muddy boots." Students read each sentence and draw a line to the correct picture. The teacher asks students to read sentences aloud and models blending sounds for words like 'sky' (/sk/ /aɪ/) and 'muddy' (/m/ /ʌdi/) as they encounter them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Spot the Weather Words (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a different short narrative about another character's rainy day adventure. Their task is to read the story together and circle all the weather-related words and items of clothing (e.g., rain, wet, puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, windy, storm). After circling, one student reads the words they found, and the partner checks if they missed any or circled incorrect ones. They also practice reading these circled words aloud, focusing on clear pronunciation of /r/ and /p/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Rainy Day Drawing and Labeling" – Students will read short descriptive sentences about rainy day activities and objects, then respond creatively by drawing and labeling. This combines reading comprehension with a creative visual response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a blank piece of paper and a set of 3-4 simple sentences about a rainy day, printed clearly. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is raining. I see a big puddle."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am wearing my raincoat and boots."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I need an umbrella because the sky is gloomy."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each sentence individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading, they draw a picture that illustrates the main idea of *all* the sentences combined. For example, a child wearing a raincoat and boots, holding an umbrella, next to a puddle, with rain falling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, students must label at least three items in their drawing using the vocabulary from the sentences and previous lessons (e.g., 'rain', 'puddle', 'umbrella', 'raincoat', 'boots', 'sky'). They practice applying phonemic awareness to spell these words correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can then share their drawings with a partner, briefly describing what they drew based on the sentences they read. (e.g., "This is my umbrella. It is raining.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Rainy Day Drawing and Labeling" activity and a quick review of student drawings and labels for comprehension and vocabulary use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "My Rainy Day Drawing and Labeling" picture will be assessed. The teacher will check if the drawing accurately reflects the main ideas and details from the provided sentences, and if at least three labels are correctly spelled and match the drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Does the drawing reflect the information from the sentences read? (e.g., Is it a rainy scene if the sentences describe rain?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least three weather-related vocabulary words (e.g., 'rain', 'puddle', 'umbrella', 'raincoat', 'boots') correctly labeled in the drawing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness/Spelling:</b> Are the labeled words mostly correctly spelled (one-syllable or two-syllable words like 'sky,' 'muddy,' 'rain')?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Creativity:</b> Does the student show a creative response to the story read?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick sentence stem or show thumbs up/down:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about _______ (rainy weather / boots)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is easy/difficult for me to find words about rain." (Students choose one)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review, self-assessment, and future planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read about rainy weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about reading the story or making your drawing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged with the illustrated narrative; the fill-in-the-blanks activity helped reinforce vocabulary.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with blending sounds for two-syllable words; I need to provide more explicit modeling for specific target vocabulary. I should pre-teach more complex vocabulary that might appear in the next story.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and communicative functions from this reading lesson to actively speak and interact in pairs about rainy weather and what they need or wear, building on their comprehension from today's story.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>